        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If a magnet grips the base firmly and the base is big enough for the zone, the pan works on induction, and most cast iron, carbon steel and clad stainless in a typical kitchen will pass. Bare aluminum, copper, glass and ceramic will not, and an interface disk is a slow stopgap rather than a fix. Weak grips, small discs and tiny bases are the grey zone where pans work badly rather than not at all.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are replacing pans, buy for the hob: fully clad stainless with a magnetic exterior, a cast iron skillet and an enameled Dutch oven cover nearly everything, quietly and quickly. Our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/">best induction cookware</a> guide has the specific pieces worth the money.
          </p>
